# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# evgen-benchmark: track the ~1 MB of cards, drivers, analysis and results.
# Everything else in this tree is generated: ~44 GB of event samples.
#
# Rule of thumb: if a generator wrote it, it is ignored; if you wrote it (or
# the analysis distilled it), it is tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- event samples and per-job output ---
# NB: match ANY *job_* directory, not just ones starting with "job_" --
# GENIE uses nucc_job_N / nnpdf_job_N, Herwig nu_job_N, Pythia me_job_N.
*job_*/
job_*/
*.hepmc
*.hepmc3
*.lhe
*.lhe.gz
*.evtfull
evtfull*
events*.lhe
# GENIE native output
*.ghep.root
*.root
*.cdat
*.status
*.gst.root

# --- generator run-time state ---
# Herwig compiled run files (~8 MB each): regenerate with `Herwig read <card>.in`
*.run
# Herwig/other per-run scratch.  NB *.tex here is HERWIG's per-run output --
# it silently swallowed the entire paper/ source once, so paper/*.tex is
# explicitly re-included below.
*.tex
*.out
Results.zip
Results.zip~
Results_*.zip
Process/
Process_*/
# Herwig integration grids (generator-written, like Sherpa's Results.zip)
Herwig-cache/
Settings_Report/
Status__*
makelibs
References.tex

# --- MG5_aMC generated process directories and its bundled tools ---
# `mg5_aMC` writes PROC_*/ and drops HEPTools/ (COLLIER, ninja, ...) into
# whatever directory it is run from.  All of it is generator output; the
# tracked artefacts are the process card, the DIS hooks and the drivers.
mg5/PROC_*/
mg5/HEPTools/
mg5/py.py
mg5/*.log
mg5/MG5_debug

# --- GENIE cross-section splines and trial output ---
# gmkspl writes these (hours of CPU, ~2 MB of XML) so by the rule above they
# are generated, not tracked.  The GENIE *config overlays* in genie/config/
# ARE tracked -- they are benchmark physics content, not generator output.
genie/splines/
genie/hedis_test/
# The nu_cc_lo overlay is REGENERATED from the installed GENIE config on every
# run (genie/make_nucc_lo_overlay.py), precisely so it cannot drift from it.
# Committing the copy would defeat that -- the SCRIPT is the tracked artefact.
genie/config/nu_cc_lo/
genie/config/nondis/

# --- FONLL component grids (yadism output, cached so the damped and undamped
#     variants do not each pay for three full runs) ---
results/fonll_cache/
results_nu/fonll_cache/

# --- logs ---
*.log
logs/

# --- compiled artefacts ---
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.dylib
__pycache__/
*.pyc
pythia8/main_dis
powheg/main_powheg
genie/gtohepmc3
nuwro/nuwro2hepmc
# Herwig's per-stem build directories: copies of herwig7/production/cards/
# plus the .run each `Herwig read` writes (lib.sh v2_compile)
herwig7/production/build/
# presentation files (talks are kept outside the public export)
*.key

# --- generated figures and reports (regenerate with plot_compare*.py and
#     make_report.py; ~20 MB per pass, so deliberately not versioned) ---
*.png
# THE PAPER'S OWN FIGURES ARE TRACKED.  Everything else a generator
# or an analysis writes is regenerable and stays out of git
# (rule 1), but a paper figure is a DELIVERABLE: a clone must build
# the document without first re-running the whole benchmark.
# tools/sync_paper_figures.sh keeps them in step with the report.
report.html

# --- the paper -------------------------------------------------------------
# The LaTeX SOURCE is tracked (it is written, not generated); the build
# artefacts are not.  The compiled PDF IS tracked deliberately, so the paper
# can be read without a TeX installation.
!paper/*.tex
!paper/*.bib
!paper/*.bst
!paper/figures/*
paper/*.aux
paper/*.toc
paper/*.out
paper/*.bbl
paper/*.blg
paper/*.synctex.gz
paper/*.fdb_latexmk
paper/*.fls

# --- editor / OS noise ---
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# --- Claude Code local settings (machine-specific permissions) ---
.claude/settings.local.json
mg5/additional_command
analysis/pineappl_cache/

# --- vendored generator EVENT files that are too big for git ---
# data/faser_flux/ and data/nomad/ are small digitised histograms and ARE
# tracked; data/forward_charm/ is 300 MB of POWHEG neutrino events from
# arXiv:2309.12793 and is not.  tools/fetch_forward_charm.sh re-fetches it in
# one command, its README carries the provenance, and the small histograms
# analysis/forward_charm_flux.py derives from it are tracked -- so a clone
# reproduces every number without the 300 MB.
data/forward_charm/*.csv.gzip
data/forward_charm/*.txt

# the FASER flux as ROOT histograms for gevgen -f (tools/genie_dimuon_ladder.sh), rebuilt in seconds
genie/faser_flux_hist/

# isospin-mirrored NEUTRON copies of the PDF sets (tools/make_neutron_pdf.py
# --all-members, 2026-09-07): 490 MB of grids rebuilt in a minute.  The
# member-0 NNPDF4.0 mirror the FASER-rate ladders were generated with stays
# tracked, as do the .info files and pdfsets.index (the ids are part of the
# recipe).
data/pdfs/lhapdf/*_n/*.dat
!data/pdfs/lhapdf/NNPDF40_nnlo_as_01180_n/NNPDF40_nnlo_as_01180_n_0000.dat

# free-nucleon TUNGSTEN averages, (74 p + 110 n)/184, from
# tools/make_isoscalar_pdf.py (2026-09-10): the NNPDF4.0 one is 157 MB of
# grids for its 101 members and is rebuilt in five seconds, so only its .info
# is tracked.  The two single-member baselines are under a megabyte each and
# stay, so a clone can reproduce the central comparison without holding
# nNNPDF3.0 and CT18A locally.
data/pdfs/lhapdf/NNPDF40_nnlo_as_01180_W184free/*.dat
# and the same for GENIE HEDIS's own set (2026-09-21): one member, 2.8 MB,
# rebuilt by tools/genie_hedis_pdf.sh in a couple of seconds.
data/pdfs/lhapdf/NNPDF31sx_nlo_as_0118_LHCb_nf_6_W184free/*.dat

# talks: Keynote render scratch
talks/.render/
!talks/style/*.png
!talks/*/recovered/*.png
~$*.pptx

# --- faser_format ---
faser_format/__pycache__/
faser_format/trace

# per-machine settings of a clone (config.sh sources it)
config.local.sh

# neutrino-telescope study: yadism structure-function grids and per-energy
# histograms, rebuilt by telescopes/yadism_sf.py (~7 min per arm) and
# telescopes/sigma_fold.py
results_telescopes/sf_cache/
